//! Output sinks for the Datastar Server-Sent Events (SSE) writer.
//!
//! Datastar drives a hypermedia UI by streaming SSE events from the server to
//! the browser. Every event is ultimately a run of already-encoded text (the
//! `event:` / `data:` lines plus the terminating blank line) that has to reach
//! somewhere. This file defines the ONE interface that the rest of the package
//! renders into: [`SseSink`], a tiny async interface with a single method,
//! [`SseSink.writeChunk`]. The event builders in the package never touch a
//! socket directly; they format a chunk of SSE text and hand it to whatever
//! sink they were given.
//!
//! Keeping that interface narrow buys two things. First, the same event-building
//! code serves a live HTTP response and a test harness without any branching:
//! swap the sink, not the caller. Second, `writeChunk` is `async`, so the live
//! path can back-pressure naturally. A slow client makes the underlying
//! [`aio.AsyncStream.sendStr`] suspend, which suspends the coroutine that was
//! producing events, so the server does not run ahead of a browser that cannot
//! keep up. There is no internal buffering or queueing here to leak or overflow.
//!
//! Two concrete sinks ship with the package:
//!
//!   1. [`StreamSink`], the production path, which writes each chunk straight
//!      to a borrowed async network stream.
//!   2. [`BufferSink`], the test and capture path, which accumulates chunks in
//!      a [`string_builder.StringBuilder`] so a test can assert on the exact
//!      bytes that would have gone on the wire, with no socket in sight.
//!
//! Both return the number of bytes accepted from the chunk, matching the
//! convention of the underlying write, so a caller can tell how much actually
//! landed.

import net.aio;
import collections.string_builder;

/// The single output interface that the Datastar SSE writer renders into.
///
/// An `SseSink` is anything that can accept one already-encoded chunk of SSE
/// text. The method is `async` on purpose: the live implementation writes to a
/// socket and must be able to suspend when the client is slow, which propagates
/// back-pressure to the event producer rather than buffering without bound.
/// Implementors return how many bytes of the chunk were accepted. See
/// [`StreamSink`] for the network path and [`BufferSink`] for the in-memory
/// capture path.
pub trait SseSink {
    /// Writes one chunk of encoded SSE text to the sink, returning the number
    /// of bytes accepted.
    ///
    /// May suspend: the network implementation awaits the underlying send, so a
    /// slow consumer parks the caller here instead of racing ahead.
    async fn writeChunk(self: SseSink, data: string): int;
}

/// An [`SseSink`] that writes each chunk straight to a live async network stream.
///
/// This is the production path: the stream is the client's HTTP response
/// connection during an SSE session. The sink only borrows the stream; it does
/// not own or close it, since the response's lifecycle is managed by the web
/// framework around it.
pub struct StreamSink impl SseSink {
    /// The borrowed async stream that chunks are written to. Typically the
    /// client's open HTTP response connection for the duration of the SSE stream.
    pub stream: aio.AsyncStream,

    /// Wraps an already-open async stream as a sink. The caller retains
    /// ownership of `s` and is responsible for its eventual close.
    init(s: aio.AsyncStream) {
        self.stream = s;
    }

    /// Sends the chunk on the underlying stream and returns the byte count it
    /// accepted.
    ///
    /// Awaits [`aio.AsyncStream.sendStr`], so this suspends until the bytes are
    /// handed to the transport; a slow client therefore back-pressures the event
    /// producer through this await instead of the package queueing internally.
    pub async fn writeChunk(self: StreamSink, data: string): int {
        return await self.stream.sendStr(data);
    }
}

/// An [`SseSink`] over the `aio.AsyncIO` trait rather than the concrete
/// `AsyncStream`.
///
/// This is what a web framework uses when it hands a streaming handler the
/// connection as an `AsyncIO` (which also covers a TLS-wrapped stream). It only
/// borrows the connection; the framework owns its lifecycle.
pub struct IoSink impl SseSink {
    /// The borrowed connection that chunks are written to.
    pub io: aio.AsyncIO,

    init(io: aio.AsyncIO) {
        self.io = io;
    }

    pub async fn writeChunk(self: IoSink, data: string): int {
        return await self.io.sendStr(data);
    }
}

/// An [`SseSink`] that accumulates every chunk in memory instead of on a socket.
///
/// This is the test and capture path. It lets a test drive the exact same event
/// builders as production and then read back the complete SSE byte stream via
/// [`BufferSink.contents`] to assert on it, with no network involved. It never
/// suspends and never applies back-pressure, so it also serves as a way to
/// render a whole response to a string in one go.
pub struct BufferSink impl SseSink {
    /// The growable buffer that every written chunk is appended to, in order.
    pub sb: string_builder.StringBuilder,

    /// Creates an empty sink with a fresh, empty backing buffer.
    init() {
        self.sb = string_builder.StringBuilder();
    }

    /// Appends the chunk to the in-memory buffer and reports its length as the
    /// bytes accepted.
    ///
    /// Always accepts the whole chunk and never suspends, so unlike
    /// [`StreamSink.writeChunk`] it applies no back-pressure. The returned count
    /// is simply `data.length`.
    pub async fn writeChunk(self: BufferSink, data: string): int {
        self.sb.append(data);
        return data.length;
    }

    /// Returns everything written so far as a single owned `string`.
    ///
    /// The result is the concatenation of every chunk passed to
    /// [`BufferSink.writeChunk`], in the order they arrived, which is exactly the
    /// byte stream that would have gone on the wire. Intended for assertions in
    /// tests.
    pub fn contents(self: BufferSink): string {
        return self.sb.toString();
    }
}
